{"step_index":3679,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T20:53:59Z","thinking":"User's agreement validates the initial assessment. The user's feedback highlights specific concerns regarding matches, particularly with the \"Cure Pharma\" example and its \"Mebo Ointment 15 mg\" listing, indicating dissatisfaction with the quality of the matching. The user's abandonment of the review after the third sheet further underscores the issue's severity.\n\n\nThe matching logic appears to be prioritizing numerical values (e.g., 15 grams) instead of matching on descriptive text (e.g., \"بيرناسور مرهم\"). This explains a mis-match with the suppositories because the system only checked \"لبوس\" without considering the drug. The suggestion to include active ingredients is irrelevant; the system's purpose is to classify drugs by warehouse inventory, not suggest alternatives. Increased filtering sensitivity is needed to improve matching precision.\n\n\nMatching must prioritize descriptive elements after initial name identification. Numerical values like concentrations and presentation (strips, etc.) serve as secondary differentiation factors when names align. The goal is to achieve a drug matching accuracy of 90-95%, focusing on improved workflow efficiency.\n\n\nMatching will now strictly enforce exact brand name matches; alternative brands are explicitly disallowed, irrespective of active ingredients. The system will now be more tolerant to packaging and minor strength discrepancies for the same drug.  The changes are intended to eliminate incorrect matches and improve the workflow efficiency.\n\n\nThe current matching approach, using character n-grams and a low score threshold, leads to a high number of irrelevant candidates being sent to the LLM. This is because numerical values in drug names are inappropriately matched, resulting in a large amount of noise. The observed behavior, where nearly all shortage items are sent to the LLM, 